+### 9. Carry a shared claim field once per scope
+
+The human page carries every claim's complete epistemic tuple and evaluation
+identity on the claim's own element. On the whole declared Butlers project
+that repetition is most of the per-item cost: as measured on 2026-09-13
+(`docs/evidence/pwb-m1-polaris-lane-a-measurement-2026-09-13.json`), the
+page after the lane A trim was 1,478,637 bytes direct and 1,484,487 bytes
+through the tailnet mount, above the 1,400,000-byte working target ruled in
+P-67 and under the 2 MiB response ceiling, with the tuple and the support
+citations the whole remaining per-item cost. The amendment lets a field whose
+value is the same for every claim under one enclosing scope be stated once
+on that scope, under one inheritance rule stated in PWB-REQ-007: a claim
+carries the nearest enclosing scope's value for any field it does not carry
+itself; a scope carries a field only when every claim under it has that
+value in the machine answer; where claims differ, each carries the field
+itself, evaluation identity included, and nothing fails to render. Claim
+identity is never carried by a scope. The machine answer never inherits and
+keeps every field on every claim. Every oracle expands scopes with its own
+statement of the rule, so the comparator still imports no rendering code,
+and PWB-REQ-020 gains the `scope-hidden` falsifier and mutant class — a
+scope value that hides one member's differing value — beside its unchanged
+`collapsed` and `duplicated` falsifiers. PWB-REQ-014's non-authority
+attributes stay on every unit: RFC7-33's non-citability sub-clause names
+them as the one field whose omission is unrecoverable at a consumer holding
+a detached unit. Rejected: an inheritance rule known only to the renderer;
+scopes that carry a majority value with members overriding it (a
+population-level assertion false for a member, with no epistemic status of
+its own); dropping tuple fields a reader is presumed not to need.
+
